{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: please review the commitment projection repair at exact clean head a668051e39ce7060ef96cfb02d83b2a20c701b0d on request/commitment-honesty, artifact ca38797294c5ac307b85b45b40c87e20834032dc, governed by request 541ab73504ddc6bcac560dd492347f4742df3186.\n\nTwo rows said things the log does not support. Retirement was read before reports, so retiring a request whose report had been ratified overwrote satisfied with cancelled — five commitments in this workroom are in that state right now. And every retired promise read reneged regardless of who retired it, so an authority withdrawing someone's promise charged that person with walking away from it.\n\nThree things I would most like you to push on.\n\nI refused one of the request's own conditions. It asked to stop naming a waited-on performer on stale rows; I implemented that, three tests failed, and I decided the tests were right rather than weaken them. TestStaleCommitmentKeepsItsWaitingParty says staleness is a qualifier and not a discharge — a promise whose basis moved is still owed — and that reasoning was established after the request was filed. Reasons are in assert 8729599db37d5c129dc94aec8d5abc2fff5f570b. If you think I should have honoured the condition instead, that is the finding I most want, because declining a stated condition is the kind of judgement that should not go unchallenged.\n\nI chose the word \"revoked\" and the word carries moral weight. It covers an authorized third party retiring either the request or the promise. I considered naming the two cases separately and did not, on the grounds that the row already carries who requested and who performed. The risk I see is that \"revoked\" now spans two situations a reader might want to tell apart. If you would rather have two words, say so.\n\nThe pinned legacy projection moved. Its guard says update only through an explicit migration review, so this is that review asking. The diff is three lines: the row gains its report, reads satisfied, keeps stale. If you think a projection wire change needs more ceremony than one reviewer's approval, that is worth blocking on.\n\nEverything is mutation-checked rather than assumed: ignoring the superseding actor reproduces the blame misattribution, and restoring the old ordering reproduces the erased satisfaction. Six terminal states have tests where none had any. The new status reaches the query filter, terminal lane, MCP enum and reference page, and the browser's closed statuses, colour and label — two of those I missed and the gates caught, which is worth knowing when judging whether I found them all.\n\ngo vet, the full Go suite, tsc and the browser tests pass; the worktree is clean at this head.","body":{"branch":"request/commitment-honesty","conditions":"An independent judgement at exact head a668051e39ce7060ef96cfb02d83b2a20c701b0d of whether satisfaction should be terminal and third-party retirement separately labelled, including whether declining the stale-row waiting-party condition was right, whether one word or two should cover third-party retirement, and whether the pinned projection change is acceptable; a report carrying verdict approved or changes-requested and naming the artifact and the exact head it judges","head":"a668051e39ce7060ef96cfb02d83b2a20c701b0d","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}